WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:11.780
DIUK: Well, good evening, everybody.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:18.480
We have a very broad subject to cover this
evening: "Russia-What to Expect from Putin?"

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.570
Subtitle: "Russia's involvement in Ukraine
and Syria, its relations with Europe and the

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.570
United States, and what to expect from President
Putin next."

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:28.570
Very broad.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:34.090
But to help us understand all of this, we
have an excellent group with us here today.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:38.399
Julie Smith works on a wide range of foreign
policy and defense issues, and is a senior

00:00:38.399 --> 00:00:45.719
fellow and director of the Strategy and Statecraft
Program at the Center for a New American Security.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:51.149
Agnia Grigas is a specialist on energy and
political risk in the post-Soviet region,

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:55.750
and a senior fellow with the Atlantic Council
and with the McKinnon Center for Global Affairs.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.219
And we have her latest book outside.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:00.890
I don't-available for purchase, or?

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:01.960
GRIGAS: (Nodding.)

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.030
DIUK: OK.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.030
(Laughter.)

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.830
And we have-(laughs)-we have with us also
the Council's very own Stephen Sestanovich,

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:15.399
George F. Kennan senior fellow for Russian
and Eurasian studies, who also teaches at

00:01:15.399 --> 00:01:18.759
Columbia University.

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:24.210
So let's start with the big picture.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:32.450
And I'm looking at Julie, who described herself
as I'm the NATO geek-(laughter)-in the group.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:38.020
So in the past two weeks we've seen-we've
seen Russian military jets swooping down awfully

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.490
close to U.S. ships in the Baltic Sea.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:46.359
There's been a meeting of the Russia-NATO
Council, an apparent ramping up of the troops

00:01:46.359 --> 00:01:48.820
in Syria after a supposed withdrawal.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:54.159
I think The Washington Post called it "zombie
ceasefire," which I actually quite liked.

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:58.979
So what are we to make of Russia's continuing
military resurgence?

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:02.700
And how will Putin respond to the upcoming
NATO summit?

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.009
Fill us in on some of these issues.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:10.520
SMITH: Well, the summer-the summer-this summer
is going to be very interesting from a Russia

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:16.659
perspective, and its relationships both with
the EU, with NATO, with Western countries

00:02:16.659 --> 00:02:18.860
like the United States.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:24.100
For one, we have the NATO summit, as you mentioned,
coming up in early July in Warsaw.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.099
That's not too far from Russia's borders.

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:30.220
And the theme of that summit is going to be
deterrence.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:35.819
The alliance spent its last summit heavily
focused on reassurance, trying to work with

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:42.019
countries in Central and Eastern Europe, and
finding ways to ensure that that they had

00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:47.400
the capabilities that they needed, and the
assets and the assistance, to help them in

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.250
the face of any potential crisis.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:56.480
Now the focus is very much on deterrence-what
more can the alliance do in, for example,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.019
the Baltic States, in Central and Eastern
Europe, to deter Russia from making any big

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:04.650
move on NATO territory?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.750
So that's one piece.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:11.840
And I think the Russians will respond poorly,
in part because of the heavy focus on deterrence,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.050
which includes emphasis on things like resilience
but also NATO nuclear policy, which I think

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.890
will get their attention.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:23.150
But I think the summit will also be a net
negative in their eyes, or they'll portray

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.340
it as such, because the alliance is enlarging.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:33.160
And while our dear friends in Montenegro really
don't pose a threat to Moscow in any way,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:39.489
shape, or form, the Russians will portray
it as another chapter in NATO's encircling-encirclement

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:47.300
of Russia, that this is another attempt for
the West to push back and contain Russia.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.450
And so we'll hear a lot of negative rhetoric.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:51.720
On the flipside, June's going to be interesting
as well.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:55.239
I would just note that we're going to be facing
a situation where the EU is going to have

00:03:55.239 --> 00:04:00.080
to vote on sanctions and determine whether
or not it wants to renew the sanctions package,

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.569
the package that's been in place because of
what's happened in Ukraine.

00:04:04.569 --> 00:04:10.700
And also, on June 23rd, our friends in London,
or across the U.K. in fact, will be voting

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.200
on whether or not to stay inside the European
Union.

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.730
And that's important from a Russian perspective
because they would love nothing more than

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:19.930
to see a weak European Union.

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:25.210
And the U.K. leaving the EU certainly would
be a bad thing, in my mind, and would definitely

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:26.850
weaken the European Union.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:33.400
So just to lay out the summer agenda, entering
kind of mid-June and running through mid-July

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.860
there are going to be a handful of events
that Russia will either view as very positive,

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:44.919
for a country that would like to see a weakened
transatlantic relationship or a weakened West,

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:48.840
but also they'll be complaining a lot because
they won't like some of the initiatives that

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:50.930
they'll see coming out of the summit.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:57.490
DIUK: Well, actually, let's take this back
a little way, maybe two years, and bring in

00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:58.490
Ukraine.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:02.370
You know, speaking of what happened this summer,
we just had the referendum in the Netherlands

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.590
that went badly for Ukraine.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:10.740
But this-you know, the military resurgence
was first noticed two years ago in Ukraine-the

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.840
annexation of Crimea, the destabilization
of Donbas.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:21.740
And I seem to remember one of the-one of the
justifications then was the NATO issue.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:25.400
But, Agnia, you were watching that very closely.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:29.460
So fill us in a little bit about Minsk II.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:34.190
Is that a set of agreements that could possibly
work in any way?

00:05:34.190 --> 00:05:37.330
Or is there some sort of broader plan?

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.580
Is Russia going to be satisfied with where
they are right now?

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:48.509
GRIGAS: Well, I think just to view Ukraine
in isolation is to miss a little bit of the

00:05:48.509 --> 00:05:51.300
big picture, and just to focus on the Ukrainian
conflict.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:59.410
Because, as you noted, although that started
in 2014, Russia's policies towards the post-Soviet

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:05.490
space-its efforts to regain influence, to
try to maintain influence, and to try to regain

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:10.169
territory-started much earlier, and actually
that is the focus of my new book.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:17.289
So there I argue and I show the evidence that,
in fact, this Russia's what I call re-imperialization

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:22.900
with an effort to try to-well, not only use
NATO's expansion as a pretext, but often trying

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:29.460
to use the issue of the so-called Russian
compatriots as a pretext, that has been driving

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:36.030
Russia's efforts to interfere in its neighboring
countries.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:41.690
Now, I'll briefly-compatriots, if you are
not familiar with the term, these are ethnic

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:47.780
Russians or Russian-speakers that reside in
the post-Soviet countries.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:55.199
And they can also even include non-ethnic
Russian-non-ethnic Russians and non-Russian

00:06:55.199 --> 00:06:57.930
speakers, but other nationalities and other
minorities.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:03.000
And they can number between 25 (million) to
150 million people, depending on how Russia

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.620
chooses to define this so-called category
of compatriots.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:12.099
So I think this issue is a longstanding issue,
and Russia will try to continuously say that

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:18.430
it will try to protect its so-called compatriots
in the territories outside of its federation.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:23.949
It has done so already in Moldova, really
in the '90s, although it was a slightly different

00:07:23.949 --> 00:07:30.250
context back then-it was the Soviet Union's
armed forces that were involved in the conflict.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:33.470
Then it happened again in Georgia in 2008.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:38.160
Many of the same policies are being pursued,
you know, and the same claims being made in

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.599
the Baltic States, in relation to Kazakhstan;
even, strangely, in relation to countries

00:07:43.599 --> 00:07:45.380
like Belarus.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.680
So I think it doesn't end with Minsk II.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.030
This is a much broader agenda of Moscow's.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:57.260
DIUK: So, Steve, what's your take on Russia's
foreign policy right now?

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:03.050
SESTANOVICH: Well, I think it's important
at this point to stand back a little bit and

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:14.010
not just monitor each week's or month's events,
as important as they are for creating a picture

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:19.530
of whether Russian policy is succeeding or
failing.

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:29.000
In the past couple of years, Russia has asserted
itself more successfully internationally than

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.729
at any time in the past 25 years.

00:08:31.729 --> 00:08:38.200
And, at the same time, it has marginalized
itself more dramatically than at any time

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:39.910
in the past 25 years.

00:08:39.910 --> 00:08:49.230
So you have these same trends-Russia, you
know, with a growing confrontation with NATO,

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.890
that people are talking about as a new Cold
War; I mean, that's been the vocabulary over

00:08:52.890 --> 00:09:01.130
the past couple of years-but it doesn't-Russians
don't see this as a stable situation.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:07.190
They're trying to figure out what's the-what's
the way that we kind of work our way out of

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:15.330
this confrontation in which there's a kind
of degree of acceptance by the West, some

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:21.960
ability to lower the temperature, some way
of unwinding the conflict.

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:28.900
If you ask Russians, you know, is Putin's
foreign policy a success, well, they'll say,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:30.700
well, Crimea's fantastic.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:39.040
But if you ask what-you know, what's the overall
record, there's a lot more uncertainty.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:46.550
And I would give you, as a bit of evidence
for this, Putin's handling of his call-in

00:09:46.550 --> 00:09:51.390
show a couple of weeks ago, which-
DIUK: I gather you watched it, so-

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.480
SESTANOVICH: I watch every year.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:54.880
I'm a big fan of it.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:10:00.230
And the dramatic theme of this one was foreign
policy is not important.

00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:04.250
You know, they kept-he kept getting questions
like, how do we take on the Americans?

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:08.200
And he would say things like, you know, the
important question is not how to take on the

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.970
Americans, it's now to address our domestic
priorities.

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:14.930
They said-another questioner said, who are
we going to hit next?

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:20.790
And he said, that isn't the question; it's
how to improve our roads.

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:24.210
You know, it's not because this is his gut
speaking.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.410
It's because this is what he-focus groups
tell him.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:33.070
So I think there's a kind of problem that
is unresolved in Russian foreign policy and

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.070
Russian politics.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:35.460
And we'll come to this, I guess, but-
DIUK: Yeah.

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:39.980
Well, I mean, there is a question as to how
far foreign policy actually is an extension

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:45.170
of-an instrument of domestic policy, but we
will come back to that.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:46.170
SESTANOVICH: Yeah.

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:50.340
DIUK: But it does actually lead to another
question, which I think Putin actually put

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:56.030
his finger on when he was talking about, you
know, mending the roads, which is that, yes,

00:10:56.030 --> 00:11:00.250
Russians are beginning to notice that their
roads are not being mended.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:04.270
Can Russia sustain the foreign policy that
it is conducting right now?

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.310
I mean, the economy is deteriorating.

00:11:06.310 --> 00:11:08.680
Oil prices are down.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:13.570
The so-called modernization of Russia's economic
infrastructure has not really been a priority

00:11:13.570 --> 00:11:14.770
up until now.

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:20.250
And Russia's expenditure on its military grew
last year to 4.2 percent of GDP, which is

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:23.410
considerably more than it had before.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:27.980
So, Agnia, I know that you follow some of
these issues quite closely in terms of the

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.260
economy.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:33.170
Where is-where is this leading us?

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:39.950
GRIGAS: Yeah, well, it's interesting you say
the-yes, military spending grew, but Russia's

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.780
GDP fell last year by almost 4 percent.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.090
So it's the same almost 4 percent.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:48.110
DIUK: So maybe the percentage is larger than-
GRIGAS: Yes.

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:49.110
(Laughs.)

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:50.110
DIUK: I see.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:55.500
GRIGAS: But, you know, can Putin really sustain
this type of foreign policy is often the question.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:00.760
And really, in the long term, no.

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:03.740
But I think in the short term it really doesn't
matter for him.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:08.270
Now, looking back at some of the previous
conflicts that I've mentioned-in Georgia and

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:17.120
Moldova-those were kind of, you know, low-cost
gains for Putin, because really there was

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.650
no significant reaction from the West.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:24.440
And with Crimea and Ukraine, this was the
first time Russia really had to pay for its

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:26.980
actions with the sanctions.

00:12:26.980 --> 00:12:31.220
And these coordinated sanctions for over two
years have had an impact on the Russian economy.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:36.190
I mean, certainly the ruble was trading at
record lows just earlier this year.

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:39.150
It was about 88 rubles to a dollar.

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:40.660
Now it's slightly improved.

00:12:40.660 --> 00:12:44.820
I mean, the inflation was soaring, double
digits last year.

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:50.540
Capital flight last year was 150 billion,
although people say the official numbers were

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:51.540
larger.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:56.690
This year, capital flight is about 50 billion
and the-and the Russians have said, well,

00:12:56.690 --> 00:13:01.220
that, oh, well, this is almost normal levels,
you know, that-(laughs)-we're improving.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:06.760
So all of these figures, you know, they're
not great indicators, and especially the negative

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:13.060
economic growth for last year of nearly 4
percent and outlook-the negative outlook for

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.060
next year.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:21.040
But what's even more important here than just
the impact of the sanctions has been the simultaneous

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:29.740
impact of the low global energy prices, specifically
low oil prices, that have dropped since 2014.

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:37.550
And this is very interesting, because if you
look back at 2008 and this-at the Russian-Georgian

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:44.890
war of the summer and August, just a month
before-in July-oil traded at record peaks,

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:47.580
$147 per barrel.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:52.840
So just last year, it was almost at record
lows, at just $27 per barrel.

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.850
So dramatic contrast.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:14:01.350
Now, for Russia, this really matters because
almost half of its budget comes from oil and

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.510
gas revenues.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:10.800
More than 70 percent of its exports-export
revenues come from oil and gas exports.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.740
So this all spells bad news.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:17.440
And more importantly, I would also stress
that it's not just-you know, we know that

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.400
there are cycles in the energy markets.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:22.320
The prices go up, they go down, similarly
like the stock markets.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:25.790
But this is something more than that because
I think that we are really also experiencing

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.520
a transformation of the global energy markets.

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:31.700
Specifically, the U.S. shale boom has left
a huge impact.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:36.310
I mean, the U.S. now is the largest oil and
gas producer in the world.

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:39.670
It overtook Russia as the largest gas producer.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:43.170
Just this year, the first U.S. LNG cargoes
left.

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:49.650
So this global energy system is really changing,
and this will hurt Russia even more long term.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:54.260
It has not even felt the full effects of this
yet, but it will.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:58.730
SMITH: I would just add to that-I think you're
absolutely right.

00:14:58.730 --> 00:15:03.930
Agree with everything you've said, particularly
on how-what kind of impact, I think, the sanctions

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:05.210
have had on the Russians.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:09.881
But I would also note that it's been very
hard to maintain those sanctions, and it's

00:15:09.881 --> 00:15:11.230
getting harder.

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:17.070
I think when the European Union looks to renew
the sanctions this summer, I think there's

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:18.980
a good chance we'll be all right.

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:23.590
And clearly, President Obama's trip had multiple
purposes in recent days.

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:28.310
One of the purposes was to stress to the Europeans
the importance of maintaining unity on the

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:29.930
sanctions policy.

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:37.210
So we will likely squeak by this summer, and
hopefully be able to maintain that policy,

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:40.930
and continue to tighten the squeeze a little
bit.

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:43.830
But, going forward, it's unclear.

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:48.940
What we're hearing from a number of our partners
in Europe is how difficult it's been for them

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:50.830
to sustain the sanctions.

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:56.450
And particularly given the fact that they
are definitely feeling the weight of the migration

00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:03.480
crisis bearing down on them, the potential
Brexit as we talked about earlier, plus some

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:08.780
weak economies in general across the European
continent, I think we're going to be in a

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:13.980
situation where at every turn this gets more
and more difficult, particularly for a country

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:16.070
like Italy.

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:20.570
Last time we were faced with a renewal, at
the end of last calendar year, the Italians

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:26.880
were the ones that asked that the actual renewal
be elevated to a higher bureaucratic level.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:32.940
It was supposed to be a fairly simple technical
rollover, and the-we got through it, but the

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:37.690
fact that the Italians paused and asked for
a bigger debate at a higher level seemed to

00:16:37.690 --> 00:16:41.650
indicate that, increasingly, these countries
are feeling the pressure.

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:46.190
So, going forward, it will just take a little
bit more effort, I think, to ensure that we

00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:52.020
can maintain that unity, assuming that we
don't see progress, for example, on Minsk.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.990
SESTANOVICH: Report from the call-in show?

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:54.990
(Laughter.)

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:55.990
DIUK: (Laughs.)

00:16:55.990 --> 00:16:56.990
Go ahead.

00:16:56.990 --> 00:17:00.660
SESTANOVICH: Putin said-told the nation the
sanctions are going to continue.

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:03.500
SMITH: That's good he has the confidence.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:04.500
(Laughs, laughter.)

00:17:04.500 --> 00:17:05.669
I'm not sure all of Europe-
SESTANOVICH: And, you know, that's expectations

00:17:05.669 --> 00:17:10.730
management, but he's not making any promises
that they're going to get out from under through

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:12.140
clever diplomacy or anything.

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:13.140
SMITH: Right, right.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:16.520
SESTANOVICH: To the contrary, he figures,
as far as the eye can see, we're looking at

00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.540
sanctions.

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.170
And there's a lot of grief that's expressed
about it.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:27.360
You know, there's a woman who stood up and
said, you know, food prices have gone up a

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.170
hundred percent in the past year.

00:17:30.170 --> 00:17:34.860
Now, I think the numbers really aren't a hundred
percent, but that's something-that's a kind

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.700
of anger that is felt.

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:42.080
The only people who like sanctions-you probably
know this, Julie-are dairy farmers.

00:17:42.080 --> 00:17:43.080
(Laughter.)

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:46.350
You know, the Russian cheese industry is booming,
but-

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:47.950
DIUK: Yeah, no more French cheeses.

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:51.140
SESTANOVICH: -but other people are-but other
people are mad.

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:58.700
And the result is a kind of pressure on Putin
to say we're listening to new ideas, we want

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:00.770
to have a strategy.

00:18:00.770 --> 00:18:08.840
He has finally managed to lure Mr. Kudrin
back into a position of semi-responsibility.

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:12.320
You know, for four-
DIUK: Now, Kudrin is a sort of liberal economic-was

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:13.320
a liberal economic reformer.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:14.700
SESTANOVICH: This is-and very-
DIUK: Tell us-tell us what the significance

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:15.700
of that is.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:22.070
SESTANOVICH: He was the finance minister,
the most-world's most respected finance minister;

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:33.370
you know, a kind of leader among the liberal
technocrats in Moscow who resigned after Medvedev

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:36.950
was appointed president in 2008.

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:46.970
And-no, I'm sorry, he resigned after-in 2011,
when it was announced that Putin was-

00:18:46.970 --> 00:18:48.100
DIUK: The switch was on, yes.

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:51.750
SESTANOVICH: -was coming back and was appointing
Medvedev as prime minister.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:58.080
But he has-Putin has been trying to get Kudrin
back into the government ever since, and there

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:03.850
is-and, you know, his friends always circulate
the rumor, "we're about to get Kudrin back,"

00:19:03.850 --> 00:19:04.940
you know.

00:19:04.940 --> 00:19:07.770
He's every-"he really is with us."

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:13.920
But he's consistently refused, and the most
he was willing to agree to was to be the director

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:19.680
of a Kremlin-funded think tank-and, in taking
the position, he said, you know, no reform

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:22.370
is going to work unless you have political
reform.

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:31.730
So it's a-it's a pretty stiff message from
the economists, the business community, and

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:36.260
from the public that these things go together,
and they're not working.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:43.990
DIUK: So that brings us to domestic politics,
which is-which is the root of how the economy

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:49.120
will be-will be conducted, how then you can
conduct foreign policy.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:51.590
But what do we have in terms of domestic politics?

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:56.570
There supposedly are parliamentary elections
coming up in September 2016.

00:19:56.570 --> 00:20:00.700
SESTANOVICH: What do you mean supposedly?

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:02.790
DIUK: Well-(laughter)-supposed parliamentary
elections.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:03.910
SESTANOVICH: Oh, I see.

00:20:03.910 --> 00:20:04.910
(Laughs.)

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:06.170
DIUK: The elections will take place.

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:13.970
But I remember someone said that elections
are a very expensive, useless ritual in Russia

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:15.400
now.

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:18.690
SESTANOVICH: That's not how Putin sees this.

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:19.690
(Laughter.)

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:21.870
DIUK: Will they give-what sort of a legitimacy
will they lend?

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:27.600
And fill us in a bit as to what the approach-what
Putin's approach to this is?

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:30.750
Because clearly he seems to be in trouble
with the economy.

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:32.090
He needs to change something.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:35.900
SESTANOVICH: Look, the first thing you have
to remember is the last parliamentary elections

00:20:35.900 --> 00:20:43.400
triggered the most significant anti-government
demonstrations that you've had since the collapse

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:45.840
of the Soviet Union.

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:49.040
And they have not gotten over that.

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:56.680
They've sort of-they worry that this process
can be mismanaged.

00:20:56.680 --> 00:21:02.790
You have half of the seats in the Duma this
time that are going to be elected in single-mandate

00:21:02.790 --> 00:21:10.650
districts, so there's less confidence that
you can rig the thing-exactly right.

00:21:10.650 --> 00:21:19.130
Putin has taken the further bizarre step of
appointing Ella Pamfilova, who is the former

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:28.250
human rights ombudsman, to be the head of
the Central Election Commission, firing his

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:34.580
old, crooked pal who had delivered the votes
time and again for him.

00:21:34.580 --> 00:21:38.680
He said, no, that just isn't going to cut
it this time; we've got to have somebody who

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:40.410
seems really clean.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:45.210
When she was appointed, people said, oh, you
know, she is just going to play along with

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:47.620
Putin, she's sold out.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:53.550
The first thing she's done is cancel the results
of elections in the Moscow suburbs just in

00:21:53.550 --> 00:22:00.020
the past week, saying, you know, actually
Navalny's people were mistreated, and this

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:01.250
won't do.

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:05.050
So you have a little bit of ferment here.

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:10.180
And the real problem for Putin is that he's
not on the ballot.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:12.500
He's popular, and he could win.

00:22:12.500 --> 00:22:19.160
But this is an occasion where he has to see
whether, not being on the ballot, he nevertheless

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:20.780
still has coattails.

00:22:20.780 --> 00:22:26.070
He made in the call-in show, if you'll allow
me one more reference to it, a kind of pitiful

00:22:26.070 --> 00:22:30.970
defense of United Russia as kind of a force
for stability.

00:22:30.970 --> 00:22:34.950
But he also promised that there would be a
dramatic increase in the number of other parties

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:36.420
represented.

00:22:36.420 --> 00:22:45.320
He sounded-he endorsed all of the angry challenges
to the-to the system, about how it's corrupt,

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:49.410
you really need to have-you need to uproot
a lot of this.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:55.370
So this is a political season in which Putin
is not sure about what the results are going

00:22:55.370 --> 00:22:56.370
to be.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.250
Now, you can overstate this.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:02.600
I think Putin is pretty confident that he's
going to come out OK.

00:23:02.600 --> 00:23:08.990
But he knows that last time it didn't go well,
and he knows that there's much more unhappiness

00:23:08.990 --> 00:23:12.870
than there was last time.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:16.800
And that's a-you know, a mix that he doesn't
like.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:24.330
DIUK: Well, we know that when opposition candidates
are allowed to run, like Alexei Navalny, he-when

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:31.360
he ran for mayor of Moscow, he did actually
get a surprising 27 percent of the vote.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:37.970
But then there are opposition politicians
who are shot dead in cold blood in front of

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:42.360
the Kremlin as well, so we have-we have a
broad spectrum.

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:51.430
And the opposition right now is not organizing
very well, but-not being given access to media-

00:23:51.430 --> 00:23:52.430
SESTANOVICH: Yeah.

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:59.280
Look, at the same time that Putin is trying
to show he's a reasonable guy, he wants to

00:23:59.280 --> 00:24:04.130
have, you know, a fair process, he wants to
hear a lot of different views, he's open to

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:13.780
new ideas, he's also allowing the, you know,
YouTube-well, no, actually, on television

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:17.750
they showed the sex tape of Prime Minister
Kasyanov.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:20.860
You know, it's kind of unbelievable.

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:27.800
There is a kind of harassment of the opposition
at the same time that there is this posture

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:35.020
of being completely open to free and fair
elections, you're absolutely right.

00:24:35.020 --> 00:24:41.180
Navalny isn't going to be on the ballot because
he's kept-he's not allowed to run anywhere.

00:24:41.180 --> 00:24:45.940
People are-there's going to be a lot of very
rough play.

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:50.890
But the ingredients of unexpected results
are there.

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:57.490
And when the Kremlin is nervous it does a
lot, both on the negative and repressive side,

00:24:57.490 --> 00:25:05.660
and on the sort of encouraging, open, pluralist
side to try to convince the public that actually,

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:08.700
you know, things are moving in the right direction.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:12.500
I would watch this-I will watch it carefully.

00:25:12.500 --> 00:25:13.500
(Laughter.)

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:17.830
GRIGAS: Well, and then even if the results
can be expected, right-even if they think

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:22.640
that they have the results under control,
they can't predict the reaction from the public.

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:27.420
And I think that's the main concern, as you
highlighted, because-precisely because of

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:31.180
the protest movements earlier, in 2011.

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:36.260
SESTANOVICH: You know, it's worth remembering
what triggered those demonstrations.

00:25:36.260 --> 00:25:41.600
It was the announcement that, you know, United
Russia had-

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:44.870
DIUK: Putin and Medvedev were going to-
SESTANOVICH: No, no, no, no, no.

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:49.940
That-it was after the elections, with the
announcement of the results that people thought

00:25:49.940 --> 00:25:53.120
were just too transparently fixed.

00:25:53.120 --> 00:25:59.000
You know, people said, well-United Russia
had gotten, essentially, 50 percent when they

00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:06.200
thought-you know, a lot of very sophisticated
Russian computer scientists were, you know,

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:09.940
crunching the vote numbers and came up with
the conclusion that in a lot of places it

00:26:09.940 --> 00:26:11.250
was 30 percent.

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:14.010
And you had an explosion.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:19.260
So, yeah, there is a problem fixing it.

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:26.200
I will say-I want to plus the current issue
of Foreign Affairs, which is absolutely-I

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:29.260
see a copy right there on this empty seat
in front.

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:31.190
Will you pick that up and-
Q: It's mine.

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:32.190
(Laughter.)

00:26:32.190 --> 00:26:33.440
SESTANOVICH: -hold is up for the whole group?

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:40.430
This is really a sensational collection of
articles that Foreign Affairs has put together.

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:47.390
But one of them that I would especially recommend
is by Masha Lipman, who says, actually, you

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:51.110
know, complete vote-rigging is not an option.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:55.880
She thinks it's that-the problem that the
Kremlin faces is more complicated than that,

00:26:55.880 --> 00:27:00.270
and so their solution has to be more complicated.

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:07.800
DIUK: Well, just quickly, before we turn to
our members here for their questions, the

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:12.570
question at the beginning of the session was:
what to expect from Putin?

00:27:12.570 --> 00:27:17.950
Very quickly, the next six months, what should
we be expecting to see from Putin?

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:22.840
What will be his most interesting move?

00:27:22.840 --> 00:27:27.330
SMITH: I don't think we've seen the end in
Syria.

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:35.600
I mean, despite having announced a withdrawal
of 30-some combat aircraft, they've taken

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.730
about, you know, half of them.

00:27:37.730 --> 00:27:44.380
We saw the insertion of heavy artillery in
recent days around Aleppo.

00:27:44.380 --> 00:27:46.960
So I'm not sure what's going to happen in
Syria.

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:52.600
I think we'll probably, in the not-too-distant
future, issue last rites on the ceasefire,

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:54.210
and then all bets are off.

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:56.100
Would Russia re-engage in Syria?

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:57.100
We don't know.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:58.100
I think it's likely.

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:02.330
I also think we'll see further acts of intimidation
across Europe, particularly in the Baltic

00:28:02.330 --> 00:28:07.410
States, the Baltic Sea, against Sweden and
Finland, against other countries in Central

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:11.100
and Eastern Europe, more buzzing of ships
like the Donald Cook.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:16.520
So I think we're in for quite a ride, and
I don't think we've seen the end on really

00:28:16.520 --> 00:28:17.900
any front.

00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:20.810
And who knows what else could open up in another
area?

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:25.360
I mean, if there's one thing we've learned
over the last couple of years is he really

00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:28.090
cherishes the element of surprise.

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:35.970
And so I'm not prepared to say, oh, we certainly
would never see this or that, because we-honestly,

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:37.110
we don't know.

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.750
But I think Syria is something to watch.

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:45.200
And particularly as we see-I hope I'm wrong,
but I think what we see is this unraveling

00:28:45.200 --> 00:28:46.300
of the ceasefire.

00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:49.290
I don't know, maybe you have something more
optimistic.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:50.290
(Laughs.)

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:53.610
SESTANOVICH: I would say, between now and
the election, downplaying of foreign policy,

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:55.340
you know?

00:28:55.340 --> 00:29:01.020
Putin wants to convey the message, and to
pacify the public with this message, that

00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:13.980
he is looking after pocketbook issues for
them and is trying to address their concern.

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:20.920
It's going to be a-you know, it's-the theme
of the election is it's the economy, stupid.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:21.920
(Laughter.)

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:22.920
You've heard that before.

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:24.480
DIUK: Sounds familiar, oh yes.

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:25.480
Agnia?

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:28.950
GRIGAS: Well, I'll take a little bit of a
longer horizon than six months.

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:34.930
But I think, longer term-let's say five, 10
years-I think, if Putin remains in power,

00:29:34.930 --> 00:29:40.520
he will remain continuously aggressive abroad
and he will be increasingly oppressive at

00:29:40.520 --> 00:29:41.840
home.

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:47.500
I think we've seen his continuous consolidation
of power-power also over the media and civil

00:29:47.500 --> 00:29:48.650
society.

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:52.130
I think Russia's becoming an increasingly
oppressive place.

00:29:52.130 --> 00:29:59.140
And even-I'd say even if Russia-if Putin leaves
the Kremlin, I wouldn't consider that everything's

00:29:59.140 --> 00:30:03.240
going to change, and that we're going to see
completely different leadership or completely

00:30:03.240 --> 00:30:04.850
different policies.

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:08.750
In terms of the work that I've done with Russia's
compatriot policies, those policies have been

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:11.870
already in place before Putin even came to
power.

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:16.330
They are greatly related to also just Russia's
historical imperial project.

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:21.520
So I would expect a bit more of the same,
but just maybe in-you know, in different-you

00:30:21.520 --> 00:30:24.900
know, in different envelopes or with different
coloring.

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:26.350
DIUK: Interesting.

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:31.240
Has he built a system that will outlive him,
as is indicated by one of the articles in

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:34.910
the Foreign Affairs-sorry, Foreign Policy-Foreign
Affairs journal?

00:30:34.910 --> 00:30:35.910
Sorry.

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:36.910
(Laughs.)

00:30:36.910 --> 00:30:37.910
SESTANOVICH: That's the name of it, yes.

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:38.910
(Laughs.)

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.420
DIUK: Mentioning the competition there.

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:46.450
But let's move on and turn it over to our
members.

00:30:46.450 --> 00:30:53.740
Please, if you would-if you have a question,
please identify yourselves and make it brief.

00:30:53.740 --> 00:30:58.250
And also, if you have a particular member
of our group that you would like to address

00:30:58.250 --> 00:30:59.450
the question to, please say so.

00:30:59.450 --> 00:31:04.880
I think we have some mics, and we'll have
the gentleman in the front here first.

00:31:04.880 --> 00:31:05.900
Q: Hi.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:07.430
Jason Rockett, Greenmantle.

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:13.059
I was wondering if you could possibly speak
about one of Putin's confidants, Vladislav

00:31:13.059 --> 00:31:15.960
Surkov, and what is his future.

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:21.970
Everything he's had his hand in, from Nashi
to Minsk II, has always seemed kind of sexy,

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:26.130
but has ultimately not really succeeded or
failed.

00:31:26.130 --> 00:31:27.800
Is Putin going to keep him around?

00:31:27.800 --> 00:31:29.130
If so, to do what?

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:36.210
What do you see him doing in the next one
to five years?

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:41.020
DIUK: Surkov.

00:31:41.020 --> 00:31:44.179
Is that a Steve question?

00:31:44.179 --> 00:31:46.230
SESTANOVICH: I-you know-
DIUK: This is a broad question about Putin's

00:31:46.230 --> 00:31:47.230
advisers.

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:48.230
SESTANOVICH: Yeah.

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:49.230
DIUK: Who are they?

00:31:49.230 --> 00:31:50.230
And do they rotate?

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:51.230
Do we know who they are?

00:31:51.230 --> 00:31:52.230
Should we expect to see others?

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:56.309
Surkov certainly has played an important role
in the last couple of years, but is he on

00:31:56.309 --> 00:31:57.309
the outs, perhaps?

00:31:57.309 --> 00:32:05.810
SESTANOVICH: It is said-I mean, I just-the
Moscow rumor mill is notoriously unreliable.

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:16.040
I think you have to bear in mind that as Putin
approaches elections, he's letting a lot of

00:32:16.040 --> 00:32:18.110
people advise him.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:22.580
He's paying a lot of money to keep people
happy.

00:32:22.580 --> 00:32:25.630
How much he's given to Surkov I don't know.

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:31.850
I would say probably less than Surkov had
hoped for last time around.

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:35.140
DIUK: Question at the front here.

00:32:35.140 --> 00:32:36.400
Q: Hello.

00:32:36.400 --> 00:32:37.670
Ben Fernandes.

00:32:37.670 --> 00:32:42.429
So it seems like Putin or Russia has continued
to conduct operations in Syria, despite they

00:32:42.429 --> 00:32:44.330
said that they pulled out.

00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:46.600
What do you think that they're trying to get
out of that?

00:32:46.600 --> 00:32:50.710
And two specific things I'm curious is, I
mean, I've heard that they want to possibly

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:57.110
try to push on NATO by trying to create tension
between Turkey and the United States; and,

00:32:57.110 --> 00:33:00.550
of course, there's the whole idea of having
a sea base on the Mediterranean.

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:03.320
So I'm curious if those things, if you think
it's something else, or what do you think

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:05.460
he's really trying to get out of Syria?

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:11.929
SMITH: Well, I think originally when they
went into Syria, they just fundamentally had

00:33:11.929 --> 00:33:13.140
two goals.

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:20.030
One, last summer, it was pretty clear that
the Assad regime was not looking that great,

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:22.290
and that they were getting increasingly nervous.

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:27.100
And so I think they deliberately reached out
and wanted assistance from Russia, and so

00:33:27.100 --> 00:33:31.110
the number-one-goal was save the regime.

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:36.970
The second goal was: maintain our military
base and presence there.

00:33:36.970 --> 00:33:41.650
And then, of course, the goal that they gave
the rest of us was we're there to fight ISIS.

00:33:41.650 --> 00:33:47.140
But as we could see, throughout most of their
campaign with the exception of the last few

00:33:47.140 --> 00:33:55.610
weeks before the withdrawal, there wasn't
that much effort in real time towards or against

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:56.610
ISIS.

00:33:56.610 --> 00:34:00.460
They were, in fact, helping the regime gain
more territory, and they were quite successful

00:34:00.460 --> 00:34:01.460
in that.

00:34:01.460 --> 00:34:08.190
The frustration for the Russians is, you know,
if you take "clear, hold," they had the clear;

00:34:08.190 --> 00:34:10.700
the Syrians were not able to hold the territory.

00:34:10.700 --> 00:34:17.450
And so there was certainly a frustration there
with the lack of capacity on the part of the

00:34:17.450 --> 00:34:21.600
Syrians, which may have, in fact, triggered
part of the decision on the withdrawal.

00:34:21.600 --> 00:34:26.840
In any case, so I think-looking back at those
two objectives, I think they felt like-Putin

00:34:26.840 --> 00:34:28.940
felt like he could check those two boxes.

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:34.080
He did save the regime, in many ways, and
he certainly was able to maintain and expand

00:34:34.080 --> 00:34:38.809
in some ways the presence that they have there,
which is very important for them in the region.

00:34:38.809 --> 00:34:45.919
And then they had kind of a very soft effort
to enhance their intel-sharing relationship

00:34:45.919 --> 00:34:47.250
with the Iraqis.

00:34:47.250 --> 00:34:51.540
But in terms of Turkey, I mean, that might
have been, you know, a side benefit in the

00:34:51.540 --> 00:34:56.030
eyes of the Russians, in the eyes of Putin,
to create more tension inside the NATO alliance,

00:34:56.030 --> 00:35:00.630
call into question the security guarantee,
call into question the viability of the alliance

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:06.890
in the face of a threat, and then portray
the Turks as irrational, unpredictable, you

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:12.140
know, throw your descriptive, you know, objective
at it-or adjective at it.

00:35:12.140 --> 00:35:19.680
But ultimately, I think going forward now
the Russians will have to make a decision,

00:35:19.680 --> 00:35:23.200
and that is can they, in fact, work with Assad
and the Iranians?

00:35:23.200 --> 00:35:24.630
Can they reach some agreement?

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:30.760
I mean, to date, the Syrians have not been
really delivering at the table on the political

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:34.550
negotiation side in ways that I think Russia
expected.

00:35:34.550 --> 00:35:38.710
The fact that the Syrians went forward with
parliamentary elections, even though the parameters

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:43.850
of the ceasefire deal said that that would
come later in the process, I think was not

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:47.200
seen as a positive step on the part of the
Russians.

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:53.810
And this is actually outlined also, I think,
in the Foreign Affairs edition here, in another

00:35:53.810 --> 00:35:57.850
article on kind of the Syrian-Russian relationship.

00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:03.460
So, yeah, looking forward, I think it's likely
that they may re-engage.

00:36:03.460 --> 00:36:06.800
For them, they'll have to determine their
objectives.

00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:09.070
What's motivating them to do so?

00:36:09.070 --> 00:36:11.470
Is it their own military needs?

00:36:11.470 --> 00:36:18.770
Is it a drive to support Assad, to help them
get a stronger hand?

00:36:18.770 --> 00:36:22.800
Is it kind of repositioning Russia vis-�-vis
the Iranians?

00:36:22.800 --> 00:36:25.480
I mean, that relationship is not watertight
either.

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:30.020
And I think we should seize on that and do
everything we can to drive a wedge there as

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:31.020
well.

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:36.150
But it's complicated in the sense that they
portray their mission in one sense and then-you

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:42.609
know, to us, the West-and then the reality
looks quite different, I think, in many ways.

00:36:42.609 --> 00:36:45.310
GRIGAS: It's also-well, it's also-just a side
note.

00:36:45.310 --> 00:36:47.500
It's interesting how they portray it domestically.

00:36:47.500 --> 00:36:51.020
And I think that the Syrian campaign, like
the Ukrainian campaign, was also an effort

00:36:51.020 --> 00:36:53.080
to create this, you know, great power posturing.

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:58.330
And it was also interesting for me to see
the rhetoric that was used in Syria by the

00:36:58.330 --> 00:37:02.530
Russian Orthodox Church and the Patriarch
Kirill, who said that Russia here is on a

00:37:02.530 --> 00:37:06.060
holy war to protect Christians.

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:09.570
So that's kind of an expansion of Russia's
mission in the world.

00:37:09.570 --> 00:37:12.920
Before it was more just about protecting,
you know, the so-called Russian compatriots,

00:37:12.920 --> 00:37:14.400
the ethnic Russians, and so on.

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:15.930
Now it's broader.

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:17.380
So that was interesting.

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:19.210
An expansion of ideology.

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:21.810
DIUK: We'll go one row behind.

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:24.110
Q: Thank you, Nadia.

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:26.710
John Sullivan with George Mason.

00:37:26.710 --> 00:37:31.170
Quick question, but a more complicated one
perhaps.

00:37:31.170 --> 00:37:36.740
When you get in trouble and you focus everything
domestically, one thing that a person in Putin's

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:41.500
position can think of doing is changing his
team, shaking it up.

00:37:41.500 --> 00:37:43.120
You've already mentioned bringing in Kudrin.

00:37:43.120 --> 00:37:46.370
What is the likelihood that Medvedev stays?

00:37:46.370 --> 00:37:50.590
And does he make other changes in his team
to say, yes, we've heard your message and

00:37:50.590 --> 00:37:55.460
here's the new team, and we're going to fix
it?

00:37:55.460 --> 00:38:04.380
SESTANOVICH: People have been writing Medvedev's
obituary from the moment Putin returned to

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:06.490
the presidency.

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:13.970
And I have to think either he just hasn't
seen the right moment for sacrificing him,

00:38:13.970 --> 00:38:23.200
or he really wants to keep him around because
he is the prime minister that Putin has total

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:30.230
confidence in, somebody who cannot usurp his
position.

00:38:30.230 --> 00:38:36.400
I will say-I said I'd made my last description
of the call-in show-(laughter)-

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:37.400
SMITH: One more.

00:38:37.400 --> 00:38:41.020
SESTANOVICH: What he said about Medvedev was
kind of cool in the call-in show.

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:43.830
It was, you know, the government is weak.

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:46.160
We need to get them tightening up.

00:38:46.160 --> 00:38:50.300
We need to crack down on corruption.

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:53.180
We need a whole series of measures.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:59.000
Surely he's hearing from Kudrin that Medvedev
is not able to deliver on any kind of reform

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:00.000
package.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:05.590
So if he wants to show he gets it, he's going
to take a new approach after the parliamentary

00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:16.451
elections, you might say that-you know, that
sacrificing Medvedev would be a good-a good

00:39:16.451 --> 00:39:19.170
approach there.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:23.530
Medvedev is his best bud.

00:39:23.530 --> 00:39:28.380
And I will be surprised the day that he gets
fired.

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:36.930
If you ask people among-you know, in Moscow,
the Moscow rumor mill that I've said is totally

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:44.940
unreliable, they think there's no chance that
there's going to be a significant new approach

00:39:44.940 --> 00:39:49.110
to economic issues until Putin is reelected.

00:39:49.110 --> 00:39:56.270
So if you're not going to do that, then what's
the advantage of having-you know, of replacing

00:39:56.270 --> 00:40:01.631
Medvedev with somebody who's going to insist-I
mean, if you wanted to go with Kudrin, for

00:40:01.631 --> 00:40:03.770
example, that would be a real message.

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:09.780
But if he's not prepared to implement anything
that Kudrin says, then you're just going to

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:11.490
have Kudrin quit after a year.

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:13.140
And he doesn't need that.

00:40:13.140 --> 00:40:14.930
DIUK: Putin's reelection is 2018?

00:40:14.930 --> 00:40:16.150
SESTANOVICH: '18, yeah.

00:40:16.150 --> 00:40:20.730
DIUK: So we're assuming Putin's reelection
in 2018, just to-

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:27.220
SESTANOVICH: I'm not assuming it, but I think
he probably has it on his calendar.

00:40:27.220 --> 00:40:28.220
(Laughter.)

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:31.710
DIUK: Let's take one more on his side.

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:36.950
Maybe in the middle here.

00:40:36.950 --> 00:40:40.859
Q: Walt Slocombe from the Atlantic Council.

00:40:40.859 --> 00:40:42.281
What do you make of the buzzing incident?

00:40:42.281 --> 00:40:45.109
And if it happens again, what should the United
States and NATO do?

00:40:45.109 --> 00:40:48.330
DIUK: The planes were awfully close.

00:40:48.330 --> 00:40:49.820
SMITH: Go ahead.

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:51.740
I'll jump in after you.

00:40:51.740 --> 00:40:57.790
GRIGAS: Well, first of all, I don't think
that was something so new, because Russia

00:40:57.790 --> 00:41:03.270
has been violating, you know, the Baltic air
space for years, even though they're NATO

00:41:03.270 --> 00:41:06.070
members.

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:10.730
Maybe what's surprising that their boldness
was, considering the events in Turkey, well,

00:41:10.730 --> 00:41:14.160
that the Russian airplane was actually shot
down not so recently.

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:20.470
But I see this as a type of continuation of
intimidation that has been going on for years

00:41:20.470 --> 00:41:22.980
and will continue going on.

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:28.980
SMITH: We just had a NATO-Russia meeting,
as you know, NATO-Russia council a couple

00:41:28.980 --> 00:41:30.819
of days ago last week.

00:41:30.819 --> 00:41:36.630
And the hope was that by turning the lights
back on and reengaging in that dialogue, which

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:41.359
has been shut down for the better part of
the last two years, we would find a venue

00:41:41.359 --> 00:41:46.780
where we could talk about these near-miss
incidents and reestablish the rules of engagement.

00:41:46.780 --> 00:41:53.420
But unfortunately, despite the fact that the
meeting went over by some length, there was

00:41:53.420 --> 00:42:02.030
no agreement and no consensus on how NATO
could serve a purpose in extending a hand

00:42:02.030 --> 00:42:03.740
to Russia on this single subject.

00:42:03.740 --> 00:42:08.070
I mean, they also talked about Afghanistan
a little bit, but essentially the meeting

00:42:08.070 --> 00:42:10.849
was designed to focus on exactly this.

00:42:10.849 --> 00:42:15.700
And if you're not going to make progress there,
we've also tried in bilateral channels to

00:42:15.700 --> 00:42:23.060
work U.S.-Russia, particularly through mil-to-mil
engagement or through DOD to their MOD.

00:42:23.060 --> 00:42:27.170
And that hasn't created huge amounts of progress.

00:42:27.170 --> 00:42:29.680
But this is one of the more pressing needs
in the relationship.

00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:34.930
I mean, there are a number of countries, particularly
in Europe, that are stating we need more engagement

00:42:34.930 --> 00:42:36.210
with Russia.

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:41.849
In some ways, they want to have the more engagement
because they know that the deterrence focus

00:42:41.849 --> 00:42:44.210
of the summit is going to create more friction.

00:42:44.210 --> 00:42:50.290
But in other ways, I think the most important
focus of engaging this-at this point, is to

00:42:50.290 --> 00:42:52.349
avoid these types of incidents.

00:42:52.349 --> 00:43:00.600
We've had too many near misses with commercial
airliner, with military ships, as we just

00:43:00.600 --> 00:43:05.930
saw, but also many incidents in the Baltic
airspace, the Baltic sea.

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:12.550
We had a just-we could go on and on-a number
of incidents that really could have ended

00:43:12.550 --> 00:43:13.550
badly.

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:17.640
So at this point, we have to-to the extent
that we're going to engage with these guys

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:20.960
in any channel, this should be top of the
pops.

00:43:20.960 --> 00:43:25.550
Not necessarily sending a signal that we're
done isolating Russia and that we're going

00:43:25.550 --> 00:43:30.020
to forget about Ukraine or we're going to
go light on sanctions, but to really get at

00:43:30.020 --> 00:43:35.890
the heart of how do we ensure that we avoid
an incident where suddenly, because Russia

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:41.760
has the transponder turned off on some aircraft
and it collides with a commercial airliner,

00:43:41.760 --> 00:43:44.230
we have a huge tragedy on our hands.

00:43:44.230 --> 00:43:47.590
So this-I don't know if we'll make progress.

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:54.380
I was hoping this might be the one subject
where NATO could really make some progress.

00:43:54.380 --> 00:44:00.930
But given how unproductive that engagement
was last week, I just don't know where we

00:44:00.930 --> 00:44:01.930
go from here.

00:44:01.930 --> 00:44:03.670
DIUK: Let's switch to this side.

00:44:03.670 --> 00:44:05.210
SESTANOVICH: Can I just add one thing?

00:44:05.210 --> 00:44:07.180
Q: What should we do if it happens again?

00:44:07.180 --> 00:44:08.180
SESTANOVICH: Sorry?

00:44:08.180 --> 00:44:09.180
Q: What should we do if it happens again?

00:44:09.180 --> 00:44:11.400
SESTANOVICH: Well-
DIUK: Go ahead, Steve.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:16.850
SESTANOVICH: You know, Julie's absolutely
right that the-you know, the line that everybody

00:44:16.850 --> 00:44:20.710
in NATO has been taking is that we need strength
and dialogue.

00:44:20.710 --> 00:44:25.400
I mean, every speech that Sandy Vershbow has
given for the past couple of months has said

00:44:25.400 --> 00:44:27.510
that.

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:34.030
And they're good speeches-(laughter)-because,
you know, they want to be able to give some

00:44:34.030 --> 00:44:41.630
cover to the NATO members that are less comfortable
with the strength part of the policy.

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:48.490
I have asked military people: What are the
different ways of responding to this?

00:44:48.490 --> 00:44:56.720
You know, are there sort of rules of engagement,
just standard operating procedures of different

00:44:56.720 --> 00:44:59.570
kinds-you know, some that are maybe a little
edgier than others, maybe?

00:44:59.570 --> 00:45:01.670
I mean, what are the choices?

00:45:01.670 --> 00:45:04.369
And I haven't gotten an interesting response.

00:45:04.369 --> 00:45:09.840
But it seems to me, that's what one ought
to be asking now, is-you know, is there something

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:15.070
about this that-is there a way of responding
to it that would actually make the Russians

00:45:15.070 --> 00:45:22.220
begin to say, wait, you know, this is-this
is going to be dangerous in exactly the way

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:23.220
that Julie says.

00:45:23.220 --> 00:45:24.220
And I don't know the answer to that.

00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:25.220
SMITH: So-oh, sorry.

00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:26.220
Go ahead.

00:45:26.220 --> 00:45:28.320
GRIGAS: No, but Turkey has responded already,
right?

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:31.340
They shot down the plane.

00:45:31.340 --> 00:45:34.190
And that didn't seem to-(laughter)-really
stop it.

00:45:34.190 --> 00:45:35.380
SESTANOVICH: Well, it's a good point.

00:45:35.380 --> 00:45:37.460
But you know, here's what I would say to that.

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:43.190
GRIGAS: No, but I'm saying-
SESTANOVICH: Putin said: We know how to respond.

00:45:43.190 --> 00:45:44.450
We're going to get you.

00:45:44.450 --> 00:45:46.460
And you know what?

00:45:46.460 --> 00:45:49.740
They didn't.

00:45:49.740 --> 00:45:57.250
SMITH: Can I just say-I mean, so, in the immediate-the
good news is we film everything.

00:45:57.250 --> 00:46:04.940
And this was a remarkably incidence where
we did not allow the bureaucratic chains to

00:46:04.940 --> 00:46:09.260
weigh down our ability to get out the facts
immediately.

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:14.750
And the fact that that video was up and out,
and the speed with which it-that's important.

00:46:14.750 --> 00:46:15.750
So that's step one.

00:46:15.750 --> 00:46:17.260
That's not going to stop them.

00:46:17.260 --> 00:46:22.340
But establishing facts on the ground has been
kind of a weakness on our side.

00:46:22.340 --> 00:46:26.369
And I think that was very well-handled in
my mind.

00:46:26.369 --> 00:46:31.130
So that's, again, not going to solve the problem
but it's a good, I'll take it, step number

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:32.130
one.

00:46:32.130 --> 00:46:35.710
Step number two, in the long term as we think
through this, assuming they're not going to

00:46:35.710 --> 00:46:40.400
stop and we're going to see more incidents,
I mean, thinking through what do you do asymmetrically?

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:45.170
So you want to ensure that the guys on the
ship don't shoot down the Russian plane, right?

00:46:45.170 --> 00:46:48.970
That I think we're in agreement that would
be a bad idea.

00:46:48.970 --> 00:46:51.730
And yet, you want to figure out how to apply
pressure.

00:46:51.730 --> 00:46:57.280
Do thinking about what do you do somewhere
else that creates a problem for them.

00:46:57.280 --> 00:47:03.980
And you can do that in lots of ways, but thinking
about something away from the immediate act

00:47:03.980 --> 00:47:09.020
of what just transpired-you know, maybe it's
for the next administration, I don't know,

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:11.550
or folks inside this administration.

00:47:11.550 --> 00:47:17.070
But to think through how do you apply pressure
in another realm I think is worth thinking

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:18.070
about.

00:47:18.070 --> 00:47:22.280
I assume somebody's thinking about that, I
hope so, somewhere inside the U.S. team.

00:47:22.280 --> 00:47:26.099
But thinking more creatively in those terms
might be a good way to go about it.

00:47:26.099 --> 00:47:30.690
DIUK: Well, we are on the record, so hopefully
someone will be watching this and taking your

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:31.690
point.

00:47:31.690 --> 00:47:32.690
SMITH: We'll see.

00:47:32.690 --> 00:47:34.030
DIUK: We have the lady at the back there.

00:47:34.030 --> 00:47:35.110
Q: Hi.

00:47:35.110 --> 00:47:37.260
Claire Casey, Garten Rothkopf.

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:41.010
I'm curious, I've heard Russian opposition
figures in Washington say that Putin's strong

00:47:41.010 --> 00:47:46.099
suit is not economic policy, that he's not
great in that area.

00:47:46.099 --> 00:47:51.150
Given that the stresses have really just exposed
major structural issues in the economy, industrial

00:47:51.150 --> 00:47:54.910
policy reforms seem often to be working at
cross purposes.

00:47:54.910 --> 00:47:58.849
What can he deliver in an economy that's just
about the economy, stupid-or the election

00:47:58.849 --> 00:48:01.099
that's just about the economy, stupid?

00:48:01.099 --> 00:48:06.040
Can he really deliver for the Russian people
and change things in the long term?

00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:13.839
SESTANOVICH: Putin's problem is not that he
hasn't taken econ 101, you know?

00:48:13.839 --> 00:48:15.000
(Laughter.)

00:48:15.000 --> 00:48:22.260
It's that the-it's just as Kudrin says, these
issues are not just structural economic questions,

00:48:22.260 --> 00:48:27.140
they are structural political questions, you
know?

00:48:27.140 --> 00:48:31.710
What the-read the Foreign Affairs articles.

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:33.640
They say it all.

00:48:33.640 --> 00:48:44.070
You know, the issues are corruption, rule
of law, you know, state power in the economy,

00:48:44.070 --> 00:48:47.870
privatizing state corporations.

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:54.400
But you do that, and the political ramifications
are huge.

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:57.900
And Putin has not really wanted to step up
to this.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:06.580
I think that's the big-you know, this is why
the liberal technocrats wring their hands.

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:11.950
They've got all of the, you know, reform plans
in the drawer ready to pull out at any time

00:49:11.950 --> 00:49:15.810
in user-friendly form.

00:49:15.810 --> 00:49:22.869
The real issue is can Putin swallow the political
implications of doing what they recommend?

00:49:22.869 --> 00:49:30.359
And their conclusion has been not now, not
this year, not next year, not the year after.

00:49:30.359 --> 00:49:31.359
Maybe after that.

00:49:31.359 --> 00:49:32.359
(Laughter.)

00:49:32.359 --> 00:49:38.260
GRIGAS: Well, also, to add on that, I think
this election about-election being about economics

00:49:38.260 --> 00:49:40.590
is rhetorically election about economics.

00:49:40.590 --> 00:49:45.180
And I'm not sure to what extent there will
be real changes, because if you look-

00:49:45.180 --> 00:49:46.589
SESTANOVICH: Oh, that's for sure, yeah.

00:49:46.589 --> 00:49:53.000
GRIGAS: And also if you look over Putin's
regime, for example, the numbers to compare

00:49:53.000 --> 00:50:04.790
from 2003 to 2013, the percentage of oil and
gas in Russia's budget revenue's dramatically

00:50:04.790 --> 00:50:06.730
increased, almost doubled.

00:50:06.730 --> 00:50:12.580
So despite all talk throughout Putin's regime,
and during a period of high oil prices, of

00:50:12.580 --> 00:50:17.770
diversifying the economy, diversifying away
from oil and gas, from reforms, that never

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:19.270
happened.

00:50:19.270 --> 00:50:24.390
And I think because in Putin's mind, his real
core interest is not the Russian economy.

00:50:24.390 --> 00:50:25.990
It's remaining in power.

00:50:25.990 --> 00:50:27.360
It's consolidating his power.

00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:31.790
Its enriching himself and his associates.

00:50:31.790 --> 00:50:37.410
And as well as working on this great Russia
model-you know, great Russia.

00:50:37.410 --> 00:50:42.520
So I think, you know, the economics will take
a, you know, backseat to that.

00:50:42.520 --> 00:50:46.599
DIUK: A question from Anya there.

00:50:46.599 --> 00:50:49.839
Q: Anya Schmemann, Council on Foreign Relations.

00:50:49.839 --> 00:50:53.980
So Putin may want the Russian people to think
that he's going to be focusing on economics,

00:50:53.980 --> 00:50:58.290
but the reality is he probably won't be able
to deliver, because his bucket of goodies

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:00.430
is running low.

00:51:00.430 --> 00:51:03.859
And the tried and true Russian playbook is
when you want to distract the Russian population,

00:51:03.859 --> 00:51:11.700
you engineer some sort of foreign policy crisis,
you-you know, you engage in activities abroad,

00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:14.720
and you bring home a victory.

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:20.760
And so-and this is mostly to Steve-why wouldn't
Putin do some sort of foreign policy-something

00:51:20.760 --> 00:51:26.000
dramatic in foreign policy in the next couple
months to ensure that the election goes his

00:51:26.000 --> 00:51:27.430
way?

00:51:27.430 --> 00:51:31.430
DIUK: Remember, things happen in August.

00:51:31.430 --> 00:51:32.430
SMITH: The election-our election.

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:36.500
SESTANOVICH: I don't think-I don't think one
should just rule that out and say, oh, Putin

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:42.200
could never do that because you're right.

00:51:42.200 --> 00:51:49.960
Foreign policy calculations are based on-often
on how they'll play out at home.

00:51:49.960 --> 00:51:58.580
I'm interested that right now Putin's message
to people in preparing the run-up to the election

00:51:58.580 --> 00:52:04.109
is: I'm not trying to flimflam you with foreign
policy victories.

00:52:04.109 --> 00:52:07.130
I get what your real concerns are.

00:52:07.130 --> 00:52:18.440
The concerns are, essentially, abuse of power,
a corrupt and intolerably rigged system.

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:22.619
So I would expect that to be the theme.

00:52:22.619 --> 00:52:32.530
But if you tell me in September that something-some
little target of opportunity has emerged,

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:35.130
I won't be stunned.

00:52:35.130 --> 00:52:40.410
DIUK: At the back there.

00:52:40.410 --> 00:52:41.859
Q: Hi.

00:52:41.859 --> 00:52:45.230
Jeremy Young with Al Jazeera's investigative
unit.

00:52:45.230 --> 00:52:47.859
I have a question that's a little bit lighter.

00:52:47.859 --> 00:52:54.390
I know Russia hosted the 2014 Winter Olympics
in Sochi, and they're also slated to host

00:52:54.390 --> 00:52:57.839
the 2018 FIFA World Cup.

00:52:57.839 --> 00:53:01.070
And I was just was wondering if you could
talk a little bit about how those events fit

00:53:01.070 --> 00:53:07.990
into Putin's foreign policy strategy, and
also whether the allegations of corruption,

00:53:07.990 --> 00:53:14.560
systemic doping, bribery, other human rights
violations-whether that puts a dent into his

00:53:14.560 --> 00:53:20.970
strategy for hosting those events, and whether
that impacts his overall success.

00:53:20.970 --> 00:53:27.860
SESTANOVICH: They don't like the suggestion
that they've heard from time to time that

00:53:27.860 --> 00:53:31.920
maybe the World Cup shouldn't be held in Russia.

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:35.250
That does not go over well.

00:53:35.250 --> 00:53:40.790
On the other hand, the response to the doping
issue has been kind of interesting.

00:53:40.790 --> 00:53:43.920
It's, we've really got to look into this.

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:47.180
I mean, this is Putin's answer to an awful
lot of things these days.

00:53:47.180 --> 00:53:49.480
We've got to look into it.

00:53:49.480 --> 00:53:50.890
I'm on that one.

00:53:50.890 --> 00:54:00.130
You know, on his call-in show-(laughter)-there
somebody had a, you know, grievous complaint

00:54:00.130 --> 00:54:07.210
about construction fraud, and ripped off,
you know, all his people lost all their money.

00:54:07.210 --> 00:54:12.230
And Putin said, can you give me the name of
the construction company and the address?

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:13.230
(Laughter.)

00:54:13.230 --> 00:54:15.350
So he's really-that's his mode these days.

00:54:15.350 --> 00:54:16.970
I'm on it.

00:54:16.970 --> 00:54:19.440
But it's true of the doping issue.

00:54:19.440 --> 00:54:26.181
Q: They had to be-for example, for the World
Cup, that they rented computers that they

00:54:26.181 --> 00:54:32.160
used to work to gain the bid, and then the
computers were erased and then sent back to

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:36.500
the company that they-there was no-they kept
no evidence of any of the communications throughout

00:54:36.500 --> 00:54:37.500
that process.

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:40.450
I mean, they'll be no repercussions for any
of that.

00:54:40.450 --> 00:54:45.180
Do you think there'll be repercussions for
the, you know, WADA report which basically

00:54:45.180 --> 00:54:51.310
said that the country was responsible for
systemic doping and that the Russian lab destroyed

00:54:51.310 --> 00:54:52.310
evidence.

00:54:52.310 --> 00:54:58.520
I mean, are we not going to see any ramifications
on a global scale for any of these, you know,

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:01.420
various potential serious violations?

00:55:01.420 --> 00:55:07.110
GRIGAS: Well, it's difficult to come up with
global ramifications, right, or responses

00:55:07.110 --> 00:55:13.870
to, you know, war, like the Russia-Georgian
War of 2008, the annexation of Crimea.

00:55:13.870 --> 00:55:18.119
So you know, it might be tough to hold Russia
accountable.

00:55:18.119 --> 00:55:19.119
I'm just throwing that out there.

00:55:19.119 --> 00:55:24.869
SESTANOVICH: I'll give you a semi-serious
answer, actually.

00:55:24.869 --> 00:55:32.010
Putin's whole system is sometimes portrayed
as one where he just gives the order to do

00:55:32.010 --> 00:55:33.830
all these terrible things.

00:55:33.830 --> 00:55:37.770
You know, get on the doping right now.

00:55:37.770 --> 00:55:42.480
And, you know, kill so-and-so in the Washington
hotel.

00:55:42.480 --> 00:55:46.690
And you know, do this and do that that's terrible.

00:55:46.690 --> 00:55:48.800
My image of it is a little different.

00:55:48.800 --> 00:55:54.580
It's that there are a lot of people out there
who know that they can do these things, and

00:55:54.580 --> 00:55:58.160
Putin will cover their tracks.

00:55:58.160 --> 00:56:02.340
He will not allow prosecutions of them.

00:56:02.340 --> 00:56:09.820
He may actually be unhappy-I think, almost
surely-is unhappy with the whole doping story,

00:56:09.820 --> 00:56:14.910
and probably is telling people, we've really
got to fix this because we can't be excluded

00:56:14.910 --> 00:56:16.420
from international sports.

00:56:16.420 --> 00:56:20.570
But is he going to let anybody pay the price
for it?

00:56:20.570 --> 00:56:28.760
Is anybody going to go to jail, lose their
job, you know, lose their livelihood?

00:56:28.760 --> 00:56:32.800
That's possible always, but that's contrary
to Putin's playbook.

00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:37.970
Putin's playbook is, we smooth it over.

00:56:37.970 --> 00:56:43.700
You know, the people who killed Sergei Magnitsky,
we cover up for them, even though Putin and

00:56:43.700 --> 00:56:47.329
his buddies gained nothing from that.

00:56:47.329 --> 00:56:57.050
The system is one in which you cover up, even
when the crimes are ones you had nothing to

00:56:57.050 --> 00:57:00.770
do with and they don't benefit you, and actually
harm you.

00:57:00.770 --> 00:57:10.349
DIUK: I think we have time for one last question,
which will be the lady in the front row.

00:57:10.349 --> 00:57:11.349
Q: Hi.

00:57:11.349 --> 00:57:12.349
Wendy Frieman.

00:57:12.349 --> 00:57:16.430
Can we switch for just a minute from Russian
elections to American elections?

00:57:16.430 --> 00:57:21.690
I'm just curious who he would like to see
elected, or not?

00:57:21.690 --> 00:57:22.690
(Laughter.)

00:57:22.690 --> 00:57:25.160
SMITH: Go ahead, Steve.

00:57:25.160 --> 00:57:31.770
SESTANOVICH: You should read this brilliant
piece by Tim Snyder in the New York Review

00:57:31.770 --> 00:57:33.109
of Books.

00:57:33.109 --> 00:57:41.050
It's called either Trump's Putin Fantasy or
Putin's Trump Fantasy, or whichever it is.

00:57:41.050 --> 00:57:42.050
(Laughter.)

00:57:42.050 --> 00:57:43.500
It is full of zingers.

00:57:43.500 --> 00:57:47.750
It is so funny and so insightful.

00:57:47.750 --> 00:57:53.369
But he basically says Putin would love to
deal with Trump, because Trump is essentially

00:57:53.369 --> 00:57:59.680
a weak figure whom he would be able to manipulate
successfully.

00:57:59.680 --> 00:58:07.570
There's a little bit of Putin mindreading
in that that I can't vouch for and I'm kind

00:58:07.570 --> 00:58:10.150
of uncomfortable with.

00:58:10.150 --> 00:58:20.040
I think if Putin wants a kind of accommodation,
some way out of this period of confrontation,

00:58:20.040 --> 00:58:27.349
he may actually be happier with somebody who's
a consensus politician here, whose actions

00:58:27.349 --> 00:58:29.010
won't be quite so controversial.

00:58:29.010 --> 00:58:34.859
He doesn't want an accommodation to be something
that's discredited and repudiated.

00:58:34.859 --> 00:58:38.680
He wants a president who can deliver.

00:58:38.680 --> 00:58:47.690
DIUK: Any further thoughts about U.S. policy
the Russia, Putin's views on any of the other

00:58:47.690 --> 00:58:48.690
candidates?

00:58:48.690 --> 00:58:53.740
SMITH: Well, he-no, the Russians, they obviously
know Secretary Clinton.

00:58:53.740 --> 00:58:56.609
And they have had some rough times.

00:58:56.609 --> 00:59:02.970
I mean, after their elections and the protests
they blamed-personally they accused Hillary

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:06.210
Clinton of meddling in the election.

00:59:06.210 --> 00:59:08.760
And things got pretty tense.

00:59:08.760 --> 00:59:14.460
And so I think they-I don't-it's so hard to
calculate.

00:59:14.460 --> 00:59:23.109
I think they would maybe assume that she would
carry on with current policy, but maybe open

00:59:23.109 --> 00:59:29.450
and review things like, you know, possibly
arming Ukrainians might be back on the table.

00:59:29.450 --> 00:59:35.490
She may end up in the same spot as President
Obama, but I think she would open up the door

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:38.450
on some of the debates, and look at things.

00:59:38.450 --> 00:59:45.349
We're not going to have a permanent U.S. presence
in Europe after this summit this summer, but

00:59:45.349 --> 00:59:47.340
maybe under a future president-
SESTANOVICH: What?

00:59:47.340 --> 00:59:49.010
SMITH: We are not, no.

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:53.810
No permanent presence-U.S. presence in Central
and Eastern Europe.

00:59:53.810 --> 00:59:55.150
SESTANOVICH: In Eastern Europe.

00:59:55.150 --> 00:59:56.160
GRIGAS: Military presence.

00:59:56.160 --> 00:59:57.170
SMITH: Yes, military.

00:59:57.170 --> 01:00:01.359
SESTANOVICH: Yeah, but the Russians aren't-they
don't-they know what heel to toe means.

01:00:01.359 --> 01:00:03.270
They know it's persistent.

01:00:03.270 --> 01:00:05.940
SMITH: It's persistent, but I think we get
their attention also-

01:00:05.940 --> 01:00:07.270
SESTANOVICH: That means-that means permanent
for them.

01:00:07.270 --> 01:00:10.010
SMITH: Building something in Poland we definitely
get their attention.

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:11.010
We're not quite there.

01:00:11.010 --> 01:00:12.010
SESTANOVICH: Yeah.

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:13.010
Yeah.

01:00:13.010 --> 01:00:14.010
SMITH: Anyways, who knows?

01:00:14.010 --> 01:00:18.260
But I think there is some assumption that
she would revisit some of the debates we've

01:00:18.260 --> 01:00:22.190
already had, and maybe tighten the screws
even more.

01:00:22.190 --> 01:00:23.190
I don't know.

01:00:23.190 --> 01:00:25.089
But it's just so hard to get-I mean, who knows?

01:00:25.089 --> 01:00:32.970
Maybe he does believe there'd be some concession
with Trump.

01:00:32.970 --> 01:00:33.970
I don't know.

01:00:33.970 --> 01:00:34.970
Hard to say.

01:00:34.970 --> 01:00:37.790
DIUK: Well, I think on that we have to come
back to the question that started this, what

01:00:37.790 --> 01:00:38.790
to expect from Putin?

01:00:38.790 --> 01:00:42.060
And I don't know that we're any the wiser
after this session.

01:00:42.060 --> 01:00:43.060
(Laughter.)

01:00:43.060 --> 01:00:44.060
SESTANOVICH: No idea.

01:00:44.060 --> 01:00:45.060
DIUK: We're where we started.

01:00:45.060 --> 01:00:49.850
We raised a lot of other additional questions.

01:00:49.850 --> 01:00:52.290
But thank you so much for a very rich discussion.

01:00:52.290 --> 01:00:55.690
It's given us a lot to think about.

01:00:55.690 --> 01:01:03.770
I appreciate the members who came and posed
questions, and listening to this fabulous

01:01:03.770 --> 01:01:04.770
session.

01:01:04.770 --> 01:01:08.160
And please do read the issue of Foreign Affairs.

01:01:08.160 --> 01:01:11.200
It will stimulate your thoughts even more.

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:13.930
And join me in thanking our group here this
evening.

01:01:13.930 --> 01:01:14.930
(Applause.)

01:01:14.930 --> 01:01:14.931
(END)

